+.BI --since-cookie " FILE"
+Like
+.BR --since ,
+anchored at the per-MDT indexes
+.I FILE
+records, which the run rewrites with the indexes it reached. This is the exact
+form of "where I got to" on a filesystem with more than one MDT, and what a
+repeated job should use.
+.IP
+If an MDT has purged past its recorded index the records in between are gone,
+and the search would return a short answer with nothing to say so; that is
+refused instead. So is an index past the end of a log that holds records,
+which is what a changelog restarted after its last user was deregistered and
+the MDT remounted looks like; once the new log grows past the recorded index
+that can no longer be seen. Every MDT is checked before the search starts,
+so a purge that reaches a later MDT while an earlier one is being read is
+not caught. Nor is one record lost to a race in the MDT: records are written
+in parallel, so a run that reaches the end of a busy log can read record N+1
+before N is complete, and the next run starts past N. Keep
+.I FILE
+outside the searched tree; the run writes it, so inside the tree it reports
+itself on every run. To accept the gap and go on, remove
+.IR FILE :
+the next run starts at the oldest surviving record. A
+.I FILE
+that does not exist is a first run and starts at the oldest surviving record;
+one that cannot be written is refused before the search prints anything.
+Exactly one path may be given: the search runs once per path and each run
+advances the anchors, so a second path would start past the end of the log.
+For the same reason
+.I FILE
+is bound to the search it was written for: it records the filesystem and the
+root it ran under, and a later run naming a different one is refused rather
+than answered from anchors that describe somewhere else. One cookie per
+repeated job, then, not one per user. A
+.I FILE
+that is not a regular file is refused too.
+.B --since
+and
+.B --since-cookie
+are two ways to say the same thing and may not be given together.
+.TP
